WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.816
A post office sells only four-cent

2
00:00:05.902 --> 00:00:07.703
and five-cent stamps.

3
00:00:07.703 --> 00:00:10.704
Which amounts can you pay exactly?

4
00:00:10.704 --> 00:00:13.020
Twelve cents: three fours.

5
00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:17.019
Thirteen: two fours and a five.

6
00:00:17.019 --> 00:00:21.021
Fourteen: one four and two fives.

7
00:00:21.021 --> 00:00:23.610
Fifteen: three fives.

8
00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:27.078
Can every amount from twelve on be paid?

9
00:00:27.078 --> 00:00:30.195
And which amounts can't be paid at all?

10
00:00:30.195 --> 00:00:31.520
Pause and guess.

11
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.836
A computer tries every amount below thirty.

12
00:00:39.836 --> 00:00:44.792
Only six amounts fail: one, two, three, six

13
00:00:44.792 --> 00:00:46.775
seven and eleven.

14
00:00:46.775 --> 00:00:50.740
From twelve on, every amount works.

15
00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:53.656
But that was only up to thirty.

16
00:00:53.656 --> 00:00:55.724
A search is not a proof.

17
00:00:55.724 --> 00:00:58.290
We want every amount, forever.

18
00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.026
Try ordinary induction

19
00:01:01.026 --> 00:01:03.651
from k cents to k plus one.

20
00:01:03.651 --> 00:01:06.651
We would need a one-cent stamp

21
00:01:06.651 --> 00:01:08.620
and there isn't one.

22
00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.870
Knowing k doesn't help.

23
00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.934
Instead, look four cents back.

24
00:01:13.934 --> 00:01:17.100
If we can pay k minus three cents

25
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.900
one more four-cent stamp pays k plus one.

26
00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:24.580
For example, with k equal to nineteen

27
00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:27.122
sixteen plus four is twenty.

28
00:01:27.122 --> 00:01:30.890
The step reaches back four cents, not one.

29
00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:35.156
Suppose we checked only one base case

30
00:01:35.156 --> 00:01:35.932
twelve.

31
00:01:35.932 --> 00:01:39.520
The step jumps four cents at a time

32
00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.430
sixteen, twenty, twenty-four.

33
00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:43.790
But thirteen

34
00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:46.896
fourteen and fifteen are never reached

35
00:01:46.896 --> 00:01:49.770
and neither are seventeen, eighteen

36
00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:50.547
nineteen.

37
00:01:50.547 --> 00:01:52.100
The chain has gaps.

38
00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.869
So one base case is not enough here.

39
00:01:56.869 --> 00:02:00.360
Check four: twelve, thirteen

40
00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:02.920
fourteen and fifteen.

41
00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.833
From twelve the step reaches

42
00:02:05.924 --> 00:02:07.663
sixteen and twenty.

43
00:02:07.663 --> 00:02:11.416
From thirteen, seventeen and twenty-one.

44
00:02:11.416 --> 00:02:15.077
From fourteen, eighteen and twenty-two.

45
00:02:15.077 --> 00:02:18.830
From fifteen, nineteen and twenty-three.

46
00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.464
Together the four chains cover

47
00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.672
every amount from twelve on.

48
00:02:23.672 --> 00:02:24.433
Why four?

49
00:02:24.433 --> 00:02:26.641
The step uses k minus three

50
00:02:26.641 --> 00:02:29.001
which must be at least twelve.

51
00:02:29.001 --> 00:02:32.426
So the step only starts at k equals fifteen

52
00:02:32.426 --> 00:02:35.700
and twelve to fifteen are checked by hand.

53
00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:38.886
This is strong induction.

54
00:02:38.886 --> 00:02:41.700
One, check the base cases

55
00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:45.244
as many as the step reaches back.

56
00:02:45.244 --> 00:02:49.309
Two, assume every case from c up to k

57
00:02:49.309 --> 00:02:50.560
not just k.

58
00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:54.289
Three, use any of them to prove

59
00:02:54.398 --> 00:02:56.578
the case k plus one.

60
00:02:56.578 --> 00:03:00.174
Four, conclude that P of n holds

61
00:03:00.283 --> 00:03:02.790
for every n at least c.

62
00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:04.643
For the stamps

63
00:03:04.643 --> 00:03:08.951
every amount from twelve to k can be paid

64
00:03:08.951 --> 00:03:13.460
so k minus three can, and so can k plus one.

65
00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.714
Strong induction also proves that every

66
00:03:17.808 --> 00:03:20.729
integer from two on is a prime

67
00:03:20.729 --> 00:03:22.990
or a product of primes.

68
00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:24.120
Take sixty.

69
00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.775
It is not prime

70
00:03:25.775 --> 00:03:29.038
sixty dots make a six by ten rectangle.

71
00:03:29.038 --> 00:03:32.382
Six and ten are both smaller than sixty

72
00:03:32.382 --> 00:03:34.910
so the hypothesis covers them.

73
00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.416
Six is two times three

74
00:03:37.416 --> 00:03:39.573
ten is two times five

75
00:03:39.666 --> 00:03:42.198
so sixty is two times three

76
00:03:42.291 --> 00:03:44.260
times two times five.

77
00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:47.183
Ordinary induction could not do this.

78
00:03:47.253 --> 00:03:49.270
Knowing about fifty-nine says

79
00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:50.731
nothing about sixty.

80
00:03:50.731 --> 00:03:52.817
The step needs smaller cases

81
00:03:52.817 --> 00:03:53.930
anywhere below.

82
00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:58.141
Your turn: three-cent and five-cent stamps.

83
00:03:58.141 --> 00:04:00.590
Which amounts can't be paid

84
00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:04.390
and how many base cases does the proof need?

85
00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:06.420
Next time: functions

86
00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.820
and the pigeonhole principle.
